WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.475
Block three.

2
00:00:01.475 --> 00:00:03.875
Towards training networks.

3
00:00:03.875 --> 00:00:08.125
On the page, this block is the section "Towards training networks".

4
00:00:08.125 --> 00:00:12.825
We have posed the problem and we know how to tell whether an answer is any good.

5
00:00:12.825 --> 00:00:22.225
The rest of this session is practical: the pieces a neural network is made of, and how you write one in PyTorch, so that Session nine can train it properly.

6
00:00:22.825 --> 00:00:28.400
One architectural idea before the code, because it is the one that keeps coming back.

7
00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:34.300
How a layer is wired decides how many weights it has, and therefore how much data it needs.

8
00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:43.700
Fully connected, which in PyTorch is nn dot Linear, and which is what Sessions eight and nine use: every input is connected to every output.

9
00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:50.250
The number of weights is the number of inputs times the number of outputs, so it grows with the size of the input.

10
00:00:50.250 --> 00:00:55.150
Give it a longer sequence or a bigger image and the layer grows accordingly.

11
00:00:55.150 --> 00:01:05.600
The alternative is local connections with shared weights: each output looks at only a small window of the input, and the same small set of weights is used at every position.

12
00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:09.825
That is convolution, and it is Session ten's whole subject.

13
00:01:09.825 --> 00:01:23.550
I am flagging it now because the comparison is the clearest demonstration in the course of a general principle: an architecture is a set of assumptions about your data, and the right assumptions are worth more than extra parameters.

14
00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:29.725
Today we use the fully connected version, which assumes nothing and therefore has to learn everything.

15
00:01:30.325 --> 00:01:37.650
A short slide to set expectations about the biological analogy, since we are about to use the word network a great deal.

16
00:01:37.650 --> 00:01:43.975
Real neurons fire discrete spikes, and information is carried in their timing as well as their rate.

17
00:01:43.975 --> 00:01:50.300
Real brains loop back on themselves constantly, with feedback from later areas to earlier ones.

18
00:01:50.300 --> 00:01:56.250
Real brains are sparsely connected: each neuron contacts a small fraction of the others.

19
00:01:56.250 --> 00:02:00.350
Our artificial units output one number, not a spike train.

20
00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:07.675
The networks in Sessions eight to ten are strictly feed-forward: information goes in one direction, with no loops.

21
00:02:07.675 --> 00:02:11.350
And a fully connected layer is the opposite of sparse.

22
00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:15.700
So the analogy is loose, and it is loose in ways that matter.

23
00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:25.800
I say this not to be pedantic but because the vocabulary invites over-reading, and you will meet claims in the literature that lean on the analogy much harder than it can bear.

24
00:02:25.800 --> 00:02:30.475
What the analogy did was historical: it suggested the architecture.

25
00:02:30.475 --> 00:02:34.425
What justifies the architecture today is that it works.

26
00:02:35.025 --> 00:02:42.850
A map of the rest of the course, in one slide, organised by the only question that matters: what does each architecture assume?

27
00:02:42.850 --> 00:02:50.325
Feed-forward networks, which are today and PHD from Session seven: assume nothing about the structure of the input.

28
00:02:50.325 --> 00:02:54.775
Every input position is treated as unrelated to every other.

29
00:02:54.775 --> 00:02:58.850
Maximum flexibility, maximum data requirement.

30
00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:07.200
Convolutional networks, Session ten: assume that nearby inputs belong together and that a pattern means the same thing wherever it occurs.

31
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:11.550
Good for images, for sequence motifs, and for contact maps.

32
00:03:11.550 --> 00:03:21.250
Recurrent networks and transformers, Session eleven: assume that the input is a sequence with an order, and provide machinery for relating distant positions.

33
00:03:21.250 --> 00:03:24.350
Signal peptides and language models.

34
00:03:24.350 --> 00:03:32.900
And then Session twelve: AlphaFold, which builds in assumptions about geometry, and diffusion models, which generate rather than predict.

35
00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:42.450
That is the whole second half of the course on one slide, and the single thread through it is that each architecture is a different bet about what structure your data has.

36
00:03:43.050 --> 00:03:47.800
Back to the smallest practical details, because Session nine needs them.

37
00:03:47.800 --> 00:03:56.925
Session seven already told you the problem with the step function: its derivative is zero almost everywhere, so gradient descent gets no signal.

38
00:03:56.925 --> 00:04:06.675
Backpropagation, which is Session nine's subject, is gradient descent applied layer by layer, and it needs the derivative of every activation on the way back.

39
00:04:06.675 --> 00:04:09.350
A step function stops it dead.

40
00:04:09.350 --> 00:04:12.100
So, the three you will actually use.

41
00:04:12.100 --> 00:04:15.500
The sigmoid, which maps to between nought and one.

42
00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:19.425
Use it for a binary output, where you want a probability.

43
00:04:19.425 --> 00:04:23.075
Tanh, which maps to between minus one and plus one.

44
00:04:23.075 --> 00:04:31.350
Same S shape, but centred on zero, which is often better behaved inside a network because the outputs do not all have the same sign.

45
00:04:31.350 --> 00:04:36.725
And the ReLU: if the number is negative, output zero, otherwise output the number.

46
00:04:36.725 --> 00:04:41.575
This is the default for hidden layers in essentially all modern networks.

47
00:04:41.575 --> 00:04:52.125
It has a kink at zero where the derivative is not defined, which sounds fatal and is not: everywhere else the slope is exactly one or exactly zero, and that is enough.

48
00:04:52.125 --> 00:04:59.200
Why the choice matters more in deep networks than shallow ones is the vanishing-gradient discussion in Session nine.

49
00:04:59.200 --> 00:05:03.575
For now: sigmoid or softmax at the output, ReLU in the middle.

50
00:05:04.175 --> 00:05:09.900
For more than two classes you need the generalisation of the sigmoid, which is the softmax.

51
00:05:09.900 --> 00:05:16.650
It takes the K raw scores coming out of your network and turns them into K positive numbers that sum to one.

52
00:05:16.650 --> 00:05:23.000
The formula is: e to the power of each score, divided by the sum of e to the power of all the scores.

53
00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:25.475
Two properties worth knowing.

54
00:05:25.475 --> 00:05:28.775
First, it never changes which class wins.

55
00:05:28.775 --> 00:05:34.925
Exponentiating is monotonic, so the biggest score before is the biggest probability after.

56
00:05:34.925 --> 00:05:39.300
Softmax does not make decisions; it makes numbers comparable.

57
00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:47.200
Second, and this is the same warning as with the sigmoid earlier: numbers that sum to one are not automatically probabilities.

58
00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:56.250
The five perceptrons on our toy problem were not trained with cross-entropy, so their softmax outputs sum to one and should not be read as confidence.

59
00:05:56.250 --> 00:06:07.900
When you do train with cross-entropy, which is Session nine, softmax outputs can reasonably be interpreted as probability estimates, and you should still check their calibration on held-out data.

60
00:06:07.900 --> 00:06:15.050
A small aside that ties things together: the entropy of the softmax output tells you how confident the model is.

61
00:06:15.050 --> 00:06:24.425
Zero bits means completely certain; log to base two of five, about two point three two bits, means it cannot tell the five classes apart at all.

62
00:06:24.425 --> 00:06:27.675
That is Session one's entropy, doing a job.

63
00:06:27.675 --> 00:06:33.700
And for two classes, softmax reduces exactly to logistic regression with a sigmoid.

64
00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:35.700
They are the same thing.

65
00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:41.625
One practical PyTorch point that catches everyone once, and costs an afternoon.

66
00:06:41.625 --> 00:06:44.950
Your model should not apply the softmax itself.

67
00:06:44.950 --> 00:06:59.050
In PyTorch, nn dot CrossEntropyLoss expects the raw scores, which are conventionally called logits, and applies the log-softmax internally, combined with the negative log-likelihood, in one numerically stable operation.

68
00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:06.050
If you apply a softmax in your model and then pass the result to CrossEntropyLoss, you have applied softmax twice.

69
00:07:06.050 --> 00:07:12.425
The model will still train, which is the cruel part, and it will train badly, and nothing will tell you why.

70
00:07:12.425 --> 00:07:16.100
The reason the library does it this way is numerical.

71
00:07:16.100 --> 00:07:28.825
Exponentiating large numbers overflows, and taking logarithms of tiny ones loses precision; doing the two operations together lets the library rearrange the arithmetic into a form that is stable.

72
00:07:28.825 --> 00:07:33.400
So the rule: model outputs logits, loss function handles the rest.

73
00:07:33.400 --> 00:07:41.300
When you want actual probabilities, for reporting or for plotting, apply softmax yourself afterwards, outside the training loop.

74
00:07:41.900 --> 00:07:44.700
And here is how a model is written.

75
00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:49.300
Three pieces, and once you have seen them you can read any PyTorch code.

76
00:07:49.300 --> 00:07:53.675
A model is a Python class that inherits from nn dot Module.

77
00:07:53.675 --> 00:07:57.850
In its constructor you create the layers and store them as attributes.

78
00:07:57.850 --> 00:08:02.900
That is how PyTorch discovers your parameters: it looks at what you stored.

79
00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:10.575
And you write a method called forward, which takes an input tensor and returns an output tensor, by calling the layers in order.

80
00:08:10.575 --> 00:08:13.175
You do not write the backward pass.

81
00:08:13.175 --> 00:08:19.325
Automatic differentiation, which you met in Session two, constructs it from your forward pass.

82
00:08:19.325 --> 00:08:29.200
To make this concrete: the entire five-perceptron classifier for our three-by-three problem is one line, nn dot Linear, nine inputs to five outputs.

83
00:08:29.200 --> 00:08:33.950
Fifty parameters: five times nine weights, plus five biases.

84
00:08:33.950 --> 00:08:37.175
And here is the check that I think makes it click.

85
00:08:37.175 --> 00:08:47.700
Take the weights from the five perceptrons we trained by hand earlier in the session, copy them into that nn dot Linear layer, and the PyTorch model makes identical predictions.

86
00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.875
Nothing has been added.

87
00:08:49.875 --> 00:08:57.850
The library is not doing anything cleverer than what you already wrote; it is doing the same arithmetic with the bookkeeping handled for you.

88
00:08:58.450 --> 00:09:01.975
Now the real model, which Session nine will train.

89
00:09:01.975 --> 00:09:11.025
The input is a protein sequence: a hundred and fifty residues, one-hot encoded as twenty numbers each, flattened to three thousand inputs.

90
00:09:11.025 --> 00:09:15.575
Then one hidden layer of sixty-four units with ReLU activations.

91
00:09:15.575 --> 00:09:19.825
Then an output layer giving five logits, one per compartment.

92
00:09:19.825 --> 00:09:21.500
That is it.

93
00:09:21.500 --> 00:09:24.800
It is Session seven's picture two, at scale.

94
00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:34.025
And I would like you to do one thing every single time you build a network, which is what the slide shows: push one batch through it untrained and check the shapes.

95
00:09:34.025 --> 00:09:39.975
A hundred and five validation proteins go in as a hundred and five by three thousand.

96
00:09:39.975 --> 00:09:43.150
A hundred and five by five comes out.

97
00:09:43.150 --> 00:09:50.775
If those are right, your wiring is right, and you have eliminated the most common class of error before you have waited for any training.

98
00:09:50.775 --> 00:09:55.250
One number to notice, because Session ten will use it against us.

99
00:09:55.250 --> 00:10:01.900
The first layer alone is three thousand times sixty-four weights, about a hundred and ninety-two thousand.

100
00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:05.525
From four hundred and eighty-six training proteins.

101
00:10:05.525 --> 00:10:08.175
Hold that ratio in your head.

102
00:10:08.775 --> 00:10:13.825
And the last experiment of the session, which is a baseline you should always have.

103
00:10:13.825 --> 00:10:20.500
Take that network, with its random initial weights, and evaluate it on the homology-aware validation set.

104
00:10:20.500 --> 00:10:22.500
No training at all.

105
00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:25.250
Accuracy nought point one eight one.

106
00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:28.975
MCC minus nought point zero two seven.

107
00:10:28.975 --> 00:10:32.025
AUROC nought point four seven four.

108
00:10:32.025 --> 00:10:33.950
Look at those three.

109
00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:40.375
With five balanced classes, chance accuracy is nought point two, and we got nought point one eight one.

110
00:10:40.375 --> 00:10:45.900
MCC of chance is zero, and we got minus nought point zero two seven.

111
00:10:45.900 --> 00:10:51.175
AUROC of chance is nought point five, and we got nought point four seven four.

112
00:10:51.175 --> 00:10:57.400
All three say the same thing: this is chance, give or take the noise of a hundred and five proteins.

113
00:10:57.400 --> 00:10:58.750
Why bother?

114
00:10:58.750 --> 00:11:05.600
Because this is what your metrics look like when nothing has been learned, on your data, with your evaluation code.

115
00:11:05.600 --> 00:11:11.500
If you ever train a model and get numbers in this region, you know the problem is upstream of the model.

116
00:11:11.500 --> 00:11:19.200
And if your untrained network scores well above chance, you have a bug, and probably a leak, and you have found it in thirty seconds.

117
00:11:19.200 --> 00:11:26.550
Session nine trains this exact network, with the same metrics and the same split, so that the comparison is honest.

118
00:11:27.150 --> 00:11:35.400
Before the summary, I want to put the central message of today in its proper context, because it would be easy to hear it as classroom pedantry.

119
00:11:35.400 --> 00:11:46.150
Kapoor and Narayanan went looking for data leakage across machine-learning-based science and found it in seventeen different fields, affecting three hundred and twenty-nine papers.

120
00:11:46.150 --> 00:11:56.575
They describe eight distinct types, from textbook errors such as having no test set at all, or preprocessing before splitting, to subtle ones of the kind we have been discussing.

121
00:11:56.575 --> 00:11:59.225
One example worth the detail.

122
00:11:59.225 --> 00:12:07.475
In a re-analysis of work predicting civil wars, complex machine learning models had been reported as beating logistic regression.

123
00:12:07.475 --> 00:12:12.075
Once the leakage was fixed, the advantage disappeared entirely.

124
00:12:12.075 --> 00:12:16.800
The headline finding of a literature was an artefact of evaluation.

125
00:12:16.800 --> 00:12:22.775
In protein science, our characteristic leak is homology, and you now know how to prevent it.

126
00:12:22.775 --> 00:12:29.525
But large models make the problem harder in a new way, and the question on the slide is a genuinely open one.

127
00:12:29.525 --> 00:12:37.900
Modern protein language models are trained on essentially all of UniProt, and structure predictors on essentially all of the PDB.

128
00:12:37.900 --> 00:12:42.250
So: how would you prove that such a model never saw your test protein?

129
00:12:42.250 --> 00:12:47.850
Think about it for a moment before I give you the answer, because the answer is uncomfortable.

130
00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:51.125
Essentially, you cannot, not from the outside.

131
00:12:51.125 --> 00:12:58.000
You can check sequence identity against the training set if the training set was published, which it often is not.

132
00:12:58.000 --> 00:13:10.725
Otherwise your only sound options are a time split, using data created after the model was trained, or a genuinely blind test like CASP, where the answers did not exist when the prediction was made.

133
00:13:10.725 --> 00:13:17.500
That is the state of the art in honest evaluation, and it is worth knowing that it is this unsatisfactory.

134
00:13:18.100 --> 00:13:21.100
Let me put the whole session on one slide.

135
00:13:21.100 --> 00:13:23.600
Pose the problem from the label.

136
00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:29.600
The label decides regression or classification, and that decides your loss and your metrics.

137
00:13:29.600 --> 00:13:31.600
Split three ways.

138
00:13:31.600 --> 00:13:36.400
Train on one, choose on the second, and look at the third once, at the very end.

139
00:13:36.400 --> 00:13:44.875
Report MCC, or precision and recall, next to accuracy, because accuracy alone flatters a model on unbalanced data.

140
00:13:44.875 --> 00:13:53.550
For regression, report RMSE and R squared next to the correlation, because correlation alone cannot tell you whether the predictions are right.

141
00:13:53.550 --> 00:14:02.150
Split proteins by homology, not at random, because a relative in the training set is a relative in the test set, and your model will use it.

142
00:14:02.150 --> 00:14:09.625
And underneath all four: evaluate on data the model has never seen, in the strongest sense of never that you can arrange.

143
00:14:10.225 --> 00:14:12.300
That is Session eight.

144
00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:16.550
Session nine opens the box we have been carefully keeping shut.

145
00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:19.275
How does a network actually learn?

146
00:14:19.275 --> 00:14:30.300
Backpropagation, the training loop, and then most of the session on the many ways training fails: vanishing gradients, learning rates, overfitting, and what regularisation does about it.

147
00:14:30.300 --> 00:14:38.050
It trains the exact network we built today, on the exact split we built today, so you will be able to see what the training bought.

148
00:14:38.050 --> 00:14:46.725
The Session eight page has all of this written out, and its "Check your understanding" section at the end is the fastest way to find out whether it stuck.

149
00:14:46.725 --> 00:14:50.775
The course notebook is linked in the box under the page's title.

150
00:14:50.775 --> 00:14:52.400
See you there.

